  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT SUN AUG 30 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains the primary feature across the area this\nweek. A weak easterly flow is supporting fair-weather cumulus\ndevelopment; however, radar remains clear. A weak surface low,\ncentered approximately 125 miles south of the southeastern\nLouisiana coast, will drift slowly westward. The NHC maintains a\nlow (30%) chance of tropical development over the next 7 days.\nWhile Central Alabama will largely remain dry, deeper moisture\nwrapping around the low supports a low (20%) chance of afternoon\nshowers and storms in southern areas. Most activity will\ndissipate by sunset, though isolated showers may linger in the\nfar southeast early this evening. Severe weather is unlikely\ngiven minimal wind shear, though sufficient instability during\npeak heating could support an isolated, stronger storm capable of\nproducing gusty winds.\n\nAs the work week progresses, an upper-level ridge will expand\neastward, steering the tropical low westward across the Gulf.\nThis will result in drier air and increased subsidence,\nmaintaining rain-free conditions for the northern half of the\narea. Diurnally driven convection will remain possible across\nsouthern areas where moisture levels stay elevated. A warming\ntrend is expected, with temperatures reaching the mid to upper\n90s by midweek. Heat Advisories are anticipated by Wednesday as\nheat indices climb to 105-107 degrees.\n\n16",
